Why Are InP Substrates Important?

Indium phosphide is a compound semiconductor material with properties suited to high-speed optoelectronic devices. It supports the development of lasers, photodetectors and modulators used in optical communication systems.

Unlike silicon, InP has a direct bandgap, making it particularly useful for light-emitting and light-detecting devices operating at telecommunications wavelengths.

AI Data Centers and Optical Transceivers Support Demand

AI infrastructure requires rapid data transmission between accelerators, switches and storage systems. Optical transceivers help move data efficiently across high-speed network connections.

InP-based laser and detector technologies can support optical modules designed for demanding data rates. As optical links evolve, demand for high-quality substrates depends on device production, optical-module architectures and manufacturing yields.

Six-Inch Wafers and Manufacturing Capacity

The report identifies 6-inch wafers as the leading substrate-size segment. Larger wafer formats can improve potential production efficiency, although maintaining crystal quality and manufacturing yield at larger diameters requires specialized process control.

Substrate suppliers must manage defect density, material uniformity, surface quality and supply reliability to meet the needs of optoelectronic device manufacturers.

Market Challenges

InP substrate manufacturing involves demanding crystal-growth processes, specialized equipment and lengthy customer qualification. Capacity constraints, material costs and the difficulty of maintaining high yields can affect supply and pricing.

